**Read:** [Aqueous corrosion — Cambridge DoITPoMS](../sources/Aqueous-Corrosion-DoITPoMS.lingua#r_3P6KCGYFDFBG1WYEJ2EZY6G7J5), passivation and control; [Corrosion control and interpretation of tests — NPL](../sources/Corrosion-and-Test-Interpretation-NPL.lingua#r_6SEQ1G67MES2K0JB6X7AEA4WD6), construction materials and localized damage. Follow the shared general-chemistry corrosion Record.

A passive surface is a dynamically maintained state, not an absence of chemical reactions. Film formation, dissolution, defect transport and mechanical removal compete. Potential, solution chemistry, temperature and prior history can alter the state. A designation such as stainless does not specify performance in every chloride, crevice or temperature condition.

**Work:** Draw active, passive and breakdown behavior on an explicitly schematic polarization plot. Explain which parts the previous monotone two-branch calculation cannot represent. Compare a freshly prepared, preconditioned and damaged surface using independent film and loss evidence. Distinguish a measured breakdown event, sustained propagation and subsequent repassivation. Avoid turning a single laboratory potential into a universal service threshold.

**Return:** [Materials engineering — structure, processing and useful behavior](../Materials-engineering.lingua#r_05BF5JM8141RKF1VT5998YFZQ9).
